            <ArticleTitle>T-cell tolerance to inhaled allergens: mechanisms and therapeutic approaches.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>769-77</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1517/14712598.8.6.769</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Specific immune response to allergens is decisive in the development of clinically healthy or allergic states.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Recent developments in the mechanisms of allergen-specific peripheral tolerance can be used for future treatment modalities.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">This review focuses on recent developments in allergen tolerance.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS/CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The balance between allergen-specific IL-10-secreting T regulatory cells (Tr1) and T helper 2 (Th2) cells appears to be decisive in the development of allergic and healthy immune response against allergens. Induction of IL-10- and TGF-beta-producing Tr1 cells, IgG4-isotype-blocking antibodies, and suppressed mast cells, basophils and eosinophils represent major components of a relatively normalized immune response after allergen-specific immunotherapy and healthy immune response to aeroallergens in sensitized individuals.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
